   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 7.3. Increased capacity of member States to set adequate wages and promote decent working time</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. The Government of the Republic of Namibia has adopted a reform towards setting a new national minimum wage:
a. In January 2021 a Wages Commission has been set to facilitate and to strengthen consultation and participation of social partners in defining a national minimum wage.
b. A roadmap was developed leading to the Ministry of Labour, Industrial Relations and Employment Creation appointing in January 2021 the members of the Wage Commission with the mandate of making recommendations on a possible NMW scheme. 
c. The report prepared by the Commission was submitted to the Minister of Labour on the 30th September 2021. In accordance to the Namibian Act No 11 of 2007.

2. In April 2020, the Government of the Republic of Namibia established a National Employment and Salary Scheme for COVID-19, in an effort to save jobs and reduce potential impact on employment due to the pandemic:
a.The Employer Wage Subsidy Program for the hard hit industries under three (3) sectors of aviation, tourism and construction. The program assisted 7,900 employers employing 65,420 employees. Employers received a cash subsidy of 17% of their total wage bill and SSC contribution holiday, or both for a period of three months subject to not retrenching workers.
b. An Affected Employees program was also set guaranteeing minimum of N$1,000 (US$70) per month for 3 months to low earners registered with the Social Security Commission. This program potentially assisted between 56,000 to 117,000 workers. 1. The ILO provided technical support towards the preparation of the various tripartite discussions documents. The ILO has also in the process shared experiences from other countries undertaking NMW discussions including Ethiopia and South Africa. The ILO supported an orientation workshop for the Wage Commission in February 2021 and also provided technical inputs into the literature review and global experiences chapter of the report by the Wage Commission.

2. The wage subsidy was part of the Namibia Economic Stimulus and Relief Package to mitigate impact of COVID-19 announced in 2020. The ILO shared documentation related to the ILO policy responses including the ILO Monitors: COVID-19 and the world of work with the Ministry of Labour, Social Security Commission and social partners. The Ministry of Labour was also part of regional and continental dialogues to design tools and response frameworks for customization by member states under the AU/NEPAD and SADC collaboration and capacity building initiatives under the Africa Regional Labour Administration Centre (ARLAC).</narrative>
   </description>
