  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 3.5. Increased capacity of member States to formulate and implement labour market programmes and employment services for transitions to decent work over the life course, with particular focus on young and older workers</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Over the biennium 2020-2021 the Government of the Republic of Namibia improved the public employment service (PES) delivery system by upgrading the online job portal, capacity building of PES staff and strengthening the institutional structure of the central job center.

1. The Integrated Employment Information System (NIEIS) was upgraded for employers and job seekers to better access to labour market information and job opportunities and talents. It includes the development of mobile app, small and medium-sized enterprises service, a labour-market informationreporting tool, and improvement of user interface for job search and matching. 

Aligning with the upgraded job portal system, IT infrastructure was also strengthened by upgrading the server and IT equipment such as laptops. 
     
Capacity building for key PES staff to adopt and use the upgraded NIEIS system and infrastructure of online service delivery was carried out. 

2. To provide the systemic and user-friendly initial service, the registration center of the Central Job Center in Windhoek was renovated. It was equipped with self-service desks with internet access, projection screen for job market and policy information, and waiting area seats. Under the Korea-ILO Technical Cooperation Fund, the ILO in collaboration with the Korea University of Technology and Education (KOREATECH) supported the job portal upgrade and capacity building for PES staff.

After carrying out  the situation analysis for public employment services and IT environment for the  job portal and establishing the masterplan for NIEIS upgrade, ILO and KOREATECH, collaborating with the Task Force comprising key staff from MLIREC, supported the development of the software and procurement of hardware for the upgrading of NIEIS(2020); trained key staff, as training of trainers, on the new system (June 2021); and supported the establishment of the new job centre in Windhoek- procurement of the IT equipment, design and partitioning of the registration centre for jobseekers and employers (August 2021). 

With respect to recovery from COVID-19, the upgrade of job portal, which allows for remote provision of services, enables  jobseekers and employers to keep using the existing and enhanced employment services during restrictions.</narrative>
   </description>
