  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 3.1. Increased capacity of member States to formulate and implement a new generation of gender-responsive national employment policies, including for youth</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Government of Namibia released the Harambee Prosperity Plan (HPP) II in March 2021. The HPP II is an Action Plan of the Namibian Government towards Economic Recovery and Inclusive Growth. Under Pillar 2 of the plan the Government of Namibia committed to ensuring that its government policies, strategic plans, programmes and ministerial budgets should include Employment Impact Assessment (EIS). In recognition that EIS is an important component of decision-making relating to economic development, HPPII will monitor, evaluate and report on the number of jobs created from prioritised projects throughout the Plan. The HPPII Detailed Implementation Plan mandates the implementation of the EIS to the Ministry of Labour, Industrial Relations, and Employment Creation (MLIREC). The Independent Evaluation of the National Employment Policy II requested to ILO by the Government of Namibia and finalized in early 2020 noted that there was no budget allocation to NEP activities, and recommended that the government should adopt pro-employment budgeting measures. The evaluation also recommended the setting of specific employment creation targets under the national budget and allocations to various government Offices, Ministries and Agencies (OMAs).

The ILO provided resources for the completion of thisindependent evaluation, published in October 2021. Furthermore, the ILO, together with MLIREC officials, provided support to the Economic Advisor to the President of Namibia on pro-employment budgeting and employment impact assessments, as well as on the results of the evaluation. The ILO also participated and made technical contributions to meetings of the High Level Panel on the Namibian Economy (HLPNE) appointed in March 2019, including the Employment Creation Seminar held in September 2019, whose findings and recommendations were published by the Government in February 2020.</narrative>
   </description>
